(   [Problem Statement]: Detail the principles of three-phase power measurement using the Two-Wattmeter Method for balanced and unbalanced loads.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Two-Wattmeter Method \(Blondel's Theorem\):) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   In a 3-phase 3-wire system, total active power is the algebraic sum of two wattmeter readings:) Tj
T*
(   $P_{total} = W_1 + W_2$.) Tj
T*
(   For balanced loads with line voltage $V_L$, line current $I_L$, and power factor angle $\\phi$:) Tj
T*
(   $$W_1 = V_L I_L \\cos\(30^\\circ - \\phi\), \\quad W_2 = V_L I_L \\cos\(30^\\circ + \\phi\)$$) Tj
T*
(   Sum: $W_1 + W_2 = \\sqrt{3} V_L I_L \\cos\\phi$.) Tj
T*
(   Difference: $W_1 - W_2 = V_L I_L \\sin\\phi$.) Tj
T*
(   Power Factor calculation: $\\tan\\phi = \\sqrt{3} \\frac{W_1 - W_2}{W_1 + W_2}$.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
